# roll_axis_test.scene
# Verifies ball rolling axis: after slip settles, the angular velocity vector (omega)
# should be perpendicular to the linear velocity. The pole (orientation up vector)
# should gradually align perpendicular to omega (the "roll axis").
#
# Uses a flat slope so terrain normal is predictable. Balls start from rest and
# roll downhill — no initial velocity — so the no-slip constraint must generate
# the correct omega from scratch.
#
# Success criteria (from roll_log):
#   - "axis=GREEN" for sustained period after landing
#   - "axis_lock=LOCKED" once pole settles perpendicular to omega
#   - omega perpendicular to velocity (dot product near zero)

physics on
text off
frames 1200
fixed_step
world -9.81 0.0 998.0

# Use ~10-degree slope: slopeX = tan(10°) = 0.176, so y = 40 + 0.176*x
# At x=500: terrain height = 40 + 88 = 128. Ball starts above that.
# Ball rolls in +X direction (downhill = increasing slope height → wrong direction)
# Actually ball rolls in -X direction (downhill = decreasing X)
flat_slope 40.0 0.176 0.0

roll_log Debug/roll_axis.log

# Camera above looking down at the slope
camera overview 500 200 500  500 130 500  0 1 0

# Ball 1: starts at rest on slope, rolls downhill purely from gravity
# Terrain at x=500: y = 40 + 0.176*500 = 128. Start at y=135 (7 above terrain)
ball slope_roll 500 135 500  4.0 20.0 8.0 0.3  0 0 0  0 0 0

# Ball 2: starts with velocity perpendicular to slope direction (cross-slope roll)
ball cross_roll 500 135 520  4.0 20.0 8.0 0.3  0 0 -200  0 0 0

# Ball 3: starts with velocity at 45 degrees to slope (diagonal roll)
ball diag_roll 500 135 540  4.0 20.0 8.0 0.3  -150 0 -150  0 0 0
